/**
@mainpage CTR Kinematics Library

A C++23 library implementing the forward and inverse kinematics of a
three-tube concentric tube robot (CTR), based on the Cosserat-theory
kinematic model of Rucker, Jones & Webster (*IEEE Transactions on Robotics*,
2010).

@image html CTR_Assembly.png "A three-tube CTR and its actuation unit" width=60%

@section main_quickstart Quickstart

@code{.cpp}
#include <ctr/CTR.hpp>
#include <iostream>

int main()
{
    using namespace ctr;

    // Three tubes, innermost first. All quantities SI (m, Pa, 1/m).
    std::array<Tube, NUM_TUBES> tubes = {
        Tube{{.OD = 0.92e-3, .ID = 0.80e-3, .E = 65e9, .G = 24.6e9,
              .ls = 190e-3, .lc = 60e-3, .u_ast = {25.0, 0.0, 0.0}}},
        Tube{{.OD = 1.10e-3, .ID = 0.97e-3, .E = 65e9, .G = 24.6e9,
              .ls = 120e-3, .lc = 80e-3, .u_ast = {10.0, 0.0, 0.0}}},
        Tube{{.OD = 1.40e-3, .ID = 1.20e-3, .E = 65e9, .G = 24.6e9,
              .ls = 90e-3,  .lc = 40e-3, .u_ast = {7.14, 0.0, 0.0}}},
    };

    // q = [β₁ β₂ β₃ | α₁ α₂ α₃]: retractions [m] (non-positive), rotations [rad].
    const blaze::StaticVector<double, 6UL> q = {-120e-3, -100e-3, -80e-3, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0};

    CTR robot(std::move(tubes), q, 1e-6);  // BVP tolerance 1e-6

    // Forward kinematics. `guess` is updated in place: keep reusing it.
    bvp_type guess{};                      // zero = valid cold start
    const FKResult fk = robot.actuate(q, guess);
    if (!fk)
        return 1;
    const blaze::StaticVector<double, 3UL> tip = robot.tipPosition();  // [m]
    std::cout << "tip: " << blaze::trans(tip);

    // Inverse kinematics: move the tip 5 mm along +y, 0.5 mm tolerance.
    const blaze::StaticVector<double, 3UL> target = tip + blaze::StaticVector<double, 3UL>{0.0, 5e-3, 0.0};
    const IKResult ik = robot.solveIK(target, 5e-4, guess);
    if (ik)
        std::cout << "joints: " << blaze::trans(ik.q);
    return ik ? 0 : 1;
}
@endcode

Link against the `ctr::kinematics` CMake target (see @ref building).
A longer, commented program is in the @ref fk_ik_demo.cpp "examples".

@section main_pages Where to start

| If you want to…                                   | Read |
|---------------------------------------------------|------|
| build the library or add it to your CMake project | @ref building |
| learn the API task by task (FK, IK, shapes, loads, tuning, troubleshooting) | @ref usage |
| know the units, frames and vector layouts         | @ref conventions |
| understand the model and the numerical methods    | @ref model |
| look up a class or function                       | ctr::CTR, ctr::Tube, ctr::FKResult, ctr::IKResult, ctr::IKOptions, and the ctr namespace |

@section main_design Design highlights

- **Honest results.** ctr::FKResult and ctr::IKResult report what actually
  happened; ctr::IKResult::converged is true only when the tip actually
  reached the target within tolerance.
- **Deterministic integration.** The backbone ODE is integrated with a
  fixed-step RK4 (default 1 mm; tip accuracy ≈ 3×10⁻¹¹ m for tabletop-scale
  robots). Determinism is a feature: the finite-difference Jacobians rely on
  truncation-error cancellation between nominal and perturbed shots.
- **Exact task Jacobian.** ctr::CTR::kinematicJacobian differentiates on the
  BVP equilibrium manifold via the implicit function theorem — not at frozen
  shooting variables.
- **No hidden global state, no LAPACK/BLAS dependency.** Only Boost.Odeint
  (header-only) and Blaze.

@section main_cite Reference

D. C. Rucker, B. A. Jones and R. J. Webster III, "A Geometrically Exact
Model for Externally Loaded Concentric-Tube Continuum Robots,"
IEEE Transactions on Robotics, vol. 26, no. 5, pp. 769-780, 2010.
*/

/**
@example fk_ik_demo.cpp

Complete runnable program: builds a Nitinol three-tube robot, solves forward
kinematics from a cold start, then uses inverse kinematics to steer the tip
to a verified-reachable target, timing both. Built as `ctr_demo`
(`./build/release/bin/ctr_demo`).
*/
